ith our environmental shaping of  what we're given in that  initial inheritance so robert would you  start us off please thank you thank you  for doing this  why do you think you are the person you  are say your personality are you shy  mental health and illness are you  depressed often or cognitive abilities  how well did you do at school when we  think about what made us who we are it's  easy to  think about those things you can see and  feel like your parents and upbringing  the school you went to your friends  what i'm going to talk about is  inherited dna differences which you  can't feel in the same way  um and so for 45 years i've been  studying the extent to which genetic and  environmental differences are  responsible for making us who we are  this research a huge area of research  has been going on for a century  looks at it describes what is in a  particular population with a particular  mix of genetic and environmental  influences it's not talking about what  could be in another population or  another time it describes what is  this book blueprint is the culmination  of my 45 years of research and i  conclude that inherited dna differences  are the major systematic force making us  who we are as individuals  that research points to the importance  of the environment  but it also shows the environment works  very differently from the way we thought  it worked it's not due to the systematic  influences that we call nurture  parenting so there's a chapter that's  called parents matter but they don't  make a difference  and that's what it's about that you know  parents obviously do matter but  differences in parenting don't make much  of a differences in children's outcome  we don't have as much control over the  development of our children because  genetics accounts for about half of the  differences between children on just  about any trait that you can imagine  so that's sort of the first part of the  book that dna matters the environment it  doesn't work in the systematic way that  we assumed it worked and so what i mean  is that if you were cloned and your  clone was reared in a different woman  grew up in a different family went to  different school your clone when it grew  up would be  very similar to you you'd notice how  similar you are physically you'd be very  similar because that clone is like an  identical twin  they'd be as similar as identical twins  reared together because rearing together  doesn't make you similar what runs in  families is dna so if you've seen this  film three identical strangers about  three identical twins they're triplets  they're clones of one another reared in  very different environments their  similarities are amazing it's a dramatic  illustration of what we have a mountain  of evidence for showing the importance  of nature and not nurture the  environment's important but it doesn't  work the way we thought it worked it's  unsystematic it's called non-shared it  makes kids in a family different from  one another so that was the first 30  years of my career and then along came  the dna revolution and we probably won't  talk about that much today but it's  going to change everything because we  can now read our dna blueprint and  predict problems and promise from birth  thank you robert  are we just our genes let's let's have  the view from angelo on this well i'm a  science journalist so  you know i'm not there doing research  and coming up with theories of my own  and my job as well as to kind of  understand the science and then convey  it to the public it's also to unpick  why scientists do what they do why they  say what they say the agendas  motivations the funding behind the  research that gets done and try to put  research in context and in some ways  give the long view and we have to  understand that this debate that we're  having now is a debate that has rumbled  on for more than a hundred years  um and it started when we talk about  nature versus nurture that phrase  originated with sir francis galton  the man who  invented eugenics he was a cousin of  charles darwin and for him this really  went to the heart of the problem how can  we distill  what portion of who we are is down to  genetics and nature  and what portion of who we are is down  to external circumstances like  environment and culture and society and  that  pitting these two things against each  other and trying to weigh up which one  has more weight than the other is an  exercise that scientists have been  doing for like i said decades many many  decades  have they got any further along  yes in some ways because we do have  more understanding of how genes work now  and the kind of work that robert does  looking at  genome-wide association studies and  looking at links when we see twins you  know how much of that is down to nature  and how much is done to nurture but i  think what is becoming  clear from having spoken to lots of  scientists who work in this field is  that these things are not two separate  things they work in tandem with each  other so when you have  um  an environmental  kind of exposure that affects your body  in quite a visceral way it even affects  the development of your brain so we know  for instance neuroscientists know that  um taxi drivers in london and we've had  this research for a long time the areas  of their brain associated with memory i  think are larger because they're  exercising that area of their brain and  that when they stop being taxi drivers  that  change starts to be reversed and so the  this idea that you know there is some  innate  portion of you that is fixed and never  changes on the day that you're born and  then there's also another part of you  that is just what culture and society  and environment and nutrition have given  you and these two things are somehow  separate i think is not really the way  to think about these things it's not  nature versus nurture it's nature and  nurture together and  we don't have the full answers yet i  would say thank you i have to say that  whenever taxi drivers boast to me about  their hippocampus getting so big because  they did london knowledge i have to tell  them that means some other bit of your  brain was reduced  probably the political sensitivity part  [Laughter]  so um simon nature nurture are we making  progress is it the right question  um well i'm glad to hear angela saying  that it's not about nature versus  nurture  because that was a kind of old debate  and i think  um this festival is all about how the  light gets in and if we're still  debating nature versus nurture it means  the light hasn't really got in  we we now all recognize that these  interact  i i was pleased to hear robert making  the case for genetics because  we do now have lots of ways of measuring  what's called heritability  whether it's through twin studies like  the ones that robert does or comparing  children with their parents or looking  at the dna itself there's a lot of ways  of measuring heritability  and there isn't really any question that  herod that traits human traits are  heritable  we can look at physical ones like height  and weight and they are  strongly heritable at least fifty  percent  but that means that there's a genetic  predisposition  interacting with environmental factors  when it comes to behavior  um you know robert said parents matter  well i would make the case a bit more  strongly that i really do think  um  early influences  from parents  from the environment on how children  um  are nurtured  are hugely important  particularly for emotional well-being  but we shouldn't be losing sight of the  genetic predisposition that is also a  driver for behavior i work in the field  of autism  so autistic kids um as you all know  struggle to socialize struggle to  communicate  and have  rather narrow sometimes called obsessive  interests autism seems to be strongly  genetic you can look at the heritability  of that  and it's some estimates are 