 are  incredibly important for uh determining  maternal behavior at least in mice for  example if you carry a mutation in this  particular gene and you're a mouse you  won't look after your pups  you know those kinds of studies  obviously do exist they they are  published they i trust them as a  scientist i look at them with the  critical eye but but that's sort of a  scientist's endeavor right  what then happens is these studies get  published and  everybody else including myself as a  human being who doesn't actually specify  and who doesn't specialize in this stuff  reads it and goes what does that mean  for me as a potential mother in the  future what does that mean what does it  mean right  um  and i think i think that's what the  debate's about basically what what you  know i it's undeniable that genes and  your genome  is the basis of who you are as a person  it's undeniable because you start off as  a single cell that is just instructed  with that information that is the basic  starting point of your life it is what  determines how you grow what shape you  are and all the rest of those things  however  we all do many things in our lives that  that aren't necessarily written in our  genes right  i don't know about you guys i don't feel  particularly comfortable in this blazer  right now but i'm wearing it because  it's socially determined that i should  be doing something like that right  you know  sometimes you get angry at people and  you think i wanna you know  but we have society that tells us things  like don't hit people that you don't  like because it's not okay  so we all learn to do things that we we  would you know arguably be going against  our predetermined genetic nature right  so the real question actually i think  that we're faced with at the moment is  in this era of increasing knowledge and  people like myself and others on this  panel spewing out more and more facts as  it were data about who we are and what  our genes say we are is actually